<PageTitle>Direct your first film — StageFold</PageTitle>
<article class="page narrow"><p class="eyebrow">A SMALL STAGE, A COMPLETE WORKFLOW</p><h1>Make the pause do some work.</h1><p>A StageFold film is a cast and a sequence of continuous shots. Each performer has a starting position, an ending position, a gesture and an expression. Captions belong to a performer for an exact time window.</p><section><h2>Your first 28 seconds</h2><ol><li>Open “The Last Elevator” in the studio. It is an original first-party film, not a user's post.</li><li>Select its last shot. Rewrite the second caption to change the ending.</li><li>Select a performer, choose the End position handle, and drag the yellow foot marker. Choose “reach” or “celebrate” to change the performance.</li><li>Apply. Play the film and watch the actor travel inside the shot. Captions appear only in their time window.</li><li>Export the offline film. Open the HTML file: its player and renderer are included. It needs no account or network.</li></ol><p>WebM is optional. The button is enabled only if the browser supports canvas capture and an actual WebM MediaRecorder codec. Recording takes the film's duration, has no audio and stops without a partial download if you hide or leave the tab. HTML remains available.</p></section><section><h2>Direct, then share</h2><p>Use 3–15 seconds per shot and at most 90 seconds per film. Give each caption at least 0.7 seconds; the same actor cannot speak overlapping lines. Fit captions redistributes their beats when you shorten a shot. Cast colors and stages use a finite original vector vocabulary, so imports cannot insert scripts or remote media.</p><p>JSON contains only scene data. Save it before trying another direction or replacing the editor. Apply keeps a valid draft in this browser; storage can be blocked or cleared, so it is not your only backup. Undo remembers up to 30 applied states in the current session.</p></section><section><h2>A real shared production</h2><ol><li>Create an account, save its one-time private recovery key, then create a shared film from the studio.</li><li>Open its production page and make a viewer or editor invitation. A collaborator signs into their own account and pastes the private one-use token.</li><li>Editors save explicit revisions. A stale save returns a conflict instead of replacing another person's work. Export the local alternative, reload, inspect changes and decide what to keep.</li><li>The owner alone publishes a saved revision, explicitly licensing it under CC BY 4.0 and confirming the rights to publish.</li><li>A different author can fork that exact immutable cut. A title-only copy cannot be republished as a new remix; change the cast, movement, timing or dialogue. The descendant includes its source hash and changed-shot comparison.</li></ol></section><section><h2>Direction prompts</h2><ul><li><b>The Last Elevator:</b> move the confident character away while their words become more certain.</li><li><b>Coffee on the Moon:</b> keep both actors still until the last reply, then let one drift across the frame.</li><li><b>The Quiet Repair:</b> replace the final gesture with stillness; see whether the pause feels like a solution.</li><li><b>Lost and Found Tomorrow:</b> shorten the third shot and lengthen the final bench conversation.</li><li><b>The Library of Almost:</b> remove the final caption and let an unfinished gesture complete the scene.</li><li><b>A Garden for Rain:</b> change a sad expression halfway through the scene by cutting to a new shot.</li><li><b>The Lighthouse Postcard:</b> make the last destination visually closer than the first.</li><li><b>One Table, Two Maps:</b> swap who speaks first without changing their positions.</li><li><b>The Spare Applause:</b> delay the celebration so it follows the final caption.</li><li><b>A Clock Takes a Walk:</b> reverse one character's path and see what their disagreement becomes.</li><li><b>Borrowing a Shadow:</b> change scale across a cut to express the character's growing confidence.</li><li><b>The Last Missing Screw:</b> have both performers reach the same space at the end.</li></ul><p>These are editorial direction exercises. They do not imply an audience, a class or customer results.</p></section></article>
